  "content": {
    "id": "p28-q20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "20",
    "prompt": "Show that in any $\\triangle ABC$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}&(b-c)\\sin A\\\\&\\quad +(c-a)\\sin B\\\\&\\quad +(a-b)\\sin C=0.\\end{aligned}$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The expression equals $0$.\n\nUse $\\sin A=ka$, $\\sin B=kb$ and $\\sin C=kc$ from the sine rule; the expanded terms cancel.",
      "worked": "By the sine rule,\n$$\\frac{\\sin A}{a}=\\frac{\\sin B}{b}=\\frac{\\sin C}{c}=k.$$\nThus $\\sin A=ka$, $\\sin B=kb$ and $\\sin C=kc$. Let $E=(b-c)\\sin A+(c-a)\\sin B+(a-b)\\sin C$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}E&=ka(b-c)+kb(c-a)+kc(a-b)\\\\E&=kab-kac+kbc-kab+kac-kbc\\\\E&=0.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 68
  },
